### Safe DOM rendering (no HTML injection)

**Rule:** Treat model output as untrusted. Prefer `textContent` (or `createTextNode`) over `innerHTML`. Only allow HTML when you *must*, and then sanitize with a strict allowlist.

```js
// Model output (UNTRUSTED)
const modelText = result.text;

// Safest: render as plain text
const el = document.getElementById("model-output");
el.textContent = modelText; // prevents XSS
```

If you need structured UI, build DOM nodes explicitly:

```js
function renderMessage(container, msg) {
  const wrapper = document.createElement("div");
  wrapper.className = "msg";

  const body = document.createElement("p");
  body.textContent = msg; // still safe

  wrapper.appendChild(body);
  container.appendChild(wrapper);
}
```

#### If you must render limited HTML
Use a well-maintained sanitizer with an allowlist (e.g., DOMPurify) and **still** avoid dangerous attributes/URLs.

```js
import DOMPurify from "dompurify";

const dirty = result.html; // UNTRUSTED
const clean = DOMPurify.sanitize(dirty, {
  ALLOWED_TAGS: ["b", "i", "em", "strong", "p", "ul", "ol", "li", "code", "pre", "a"],
  ALLOWED_ATTR: ["href", "title"],
  ALLOW_UNKNOWN_PROTOCOLS: false,
});

const el = document.getElementById("model-output");
el.innerHTML = clean;
```

Also validate links before allowing them:

```js
function isSafeHttpUrl(url) {
  try {
    const u = new URL(url, window.location.origin);
    return u.protocol === "http:" || u.protocol === "https:";
  } catch { return false; }
}
```

